# Lesson 11: SOLID Principles

SOLID is a set of five object-oriented design principles that help us write code that is:

* Easy to understand
* Easy to maintain
* Easy to test
* Flexible and extensible
* Loosely coupled

## SOLID at a Glance

| Principle   | Meaning                                                                                   |
| ----------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| **S — SRP** | Single Responsibility Principle — One responsibility / one reason to change               |
| **O — OCP** | Open/Closed Principle — Open for extension, closed for modification                       |
| **L — LSP** | Liskov Substitution Principle — Child should safely replace parent                        |
| **I — ISP** | Interface Segregation Principle — Don't force classes to depend on things they don't need |
| **D — DIP** | Dependency Inversion Principle — Depend on abstractions, not concrete implementations     |

---

# 1. S — Single Responsibility Principle (SRP)

> **A class should have one clear responsibility and one reason to change.**

The goal of SRP is to avoid putting unrelated responsibilities into a single class.

### ❌ Bad Example

```csharp
public class Employee
{
    public void CalculateSalary()
    {
        // Salary calculation
    }

    public void SaveToDatabase()
    {
        // Database logic
    }

    public void SendEmail()
    {
        // Email logic
    }
}
```

Here, `Employee` has multiple responsibilities:

* Salary calculation
* Database persistence
* Sending emails

This means the class has multiple reasons to change.

### ✅ Better Example

```csharp
public class SalaryCalculator
{
    public decimal CalculateSalary()
    {
        // Salary calculation
        return 50000;
    }
}

public class EmployeeRepository
{
    public void Save()
    {
        // Database logic
    }
}

public class EmailService
{
    public void SendEmail()
    {
        // Email logic
    }
}
```

Now each class has one clear responsibility.

### 🧠 Mental Model

```text
One Class
    ↓
One Responsibility
    ↓
One Reason to Change
```

