# 5. D — Dependency Inversion Principle (DIP)

> **Depend on abstractions, not concrete implementations.**

High-level modules should not directly depend on low-level modules.

Instead, both should depend on abstractions.

## ❌ Without DIP

```csharp
public class EmailService
{
    public void Send()
    {
        Console.WriteLine("Sending email...");
    }
}

public class OrderService
{
    private EmailService _emailService = new EmailService();

    public void PlaceOrder()
    {
        // Place order

        _emailService.Send();
    }
}
```

`OrderService` is directly dependent on the concrete `EmailService`.

If later we want to use SMS instead of email, we need to modify `OrderService`.

## ✅ Using DIP

Create an abstraction:

```csharp
public interface INotificationService
{
    void Send();
}
```

Implement it:

```csharp
public class EmailService : INotificationService
{
    public void Send()
    {
        Console.WriteLine("Sending email...");
    }
}
```

Another implementation:

```csharp
public class SmsService : INotificationService
{
    public void Send()
    {
        Console.WriteLine("Sending SMS...");
    }
}
```

Now `OrderService` depends on the abstraction:

```csharp
public class OrderService
{
    private readonly INotificationService _notificationService;

    public OrderService(INotificationService notificationService)
    {
        _notificationService = notificationService;
    }

    public void PlaceOrder()
    {
        // Place order

        _notificationService.Send();
    }
}
```

### 🧠 Mental Model

```text
❌ DIP Violation

OrderService
      │
      ↓
EmailService


✅ DIP

OrderService
      │
      ↓
INotificationService
      ↑
      │
 ┌────┴─────┐
 ↓          ↓
Email      SMS
```

